Ladybird measures the rows of every table from its cells laid out at
the used widths of their columns, and never from a cell’s block size
at its max-content inline size. Blink’s ComputeMinimumRowBlockSize()
lays out each cell at its column’s width (Layout() with a constraint
space from the column locations) before it takes the row’s minimum.
WebKit’s RenderTableSection::calcRowLogicalHeight() lays out each cell
(layoutIfNeeded()) and uses logicalHeightForRowSizing(). And Gecko’s
nsTableRowGroupFrame::CalculateRowBSizes() works from rows whose cells
were already reflowed at their column widths.

In Chrome, every case in the test comes out the same as in Ladybird.
